  "text": "The issue of how disturbances in the gravitational field travel was first examined by the pioneering relativist and later revisited. Although it is often taken for granted that a shift in matter distribution sends out effects at light speed, the original work never firmly fixed that speed. The analysis shows that one can pick a coordinate description that makes the potentials appear to move at light speed, but this choice is arbitrary and does not prove that light speed is inherent to the phenomenon. To date, the actual transmission of the changed space‑time curvature has not been treated directly. A later mathematician sorted possible planar gravitational ripples into three categories: fully longitudinal, mixed longitudinal‑transverse, and fully transverse. The study finds that only the fully transverse ripples travel at light speed in any coordinate system, while the other two types lack a definite speed, casting doubt on their physical reality. Moreover, these non‑transverse patterns carry no energy and are regarded as mere coordinate artifacts, detectable only in theory, not experiment."
